Can I copy venv inside marketpulse?

Apr 19
Yes! Since you already installed all packages in the venv outside, just move/copy the folder:

powershell
# Copy venv into marketpulse
Copy-Item -Recurse "path\to\your\venv" "marketpulse\venv"
Then reactivate it from inside the project:

powershell
cd marketpulse
venv\Scripts\activate
However — a cleaner approach is to recreate it inside the project:

powershell
cd marketpulse

# Create fresh venv
python -m venv venv

# Activate it
venv\Scripts\activate

# Install all packages
pip install fastapi uvicorn kafka-python pandas numpy yfinance sqlalchemy requests websockets openai python-dotenv
Why recreate instead of copy?

venv folders contain absolute paths — copying them from another location can break things silently
Fresh install takes only 1-2 minutes and is guaranteed to work
Cleaner for when you push to GitHub (you'll .gitignore the venv anyway)
I'd recommend the fresh install. Want me to also generate a requirements.txt so you never have to remember the package list again?

1776587742985_image.png
Apr 19
This is VS Code telling you it found your .env file but isn't auto-loading it into the terminal. Fix it in two steps:

1. Press Ctrl+Shift+P → type Open User Settings JSON → select it

2. Add this line inside the JSON:

json
"python.terminal.useEnvFile": true
It should look like this:

json
{
    "python.terminal.useEnvFile": true,
    // ... your other settings
}
3. Restart the VS Code terminal (Ctrl+``  then close and reopen)

This tells VS Code to automatically inject your .env variables into every terminal session — so you won't need to manually load them in your Python scripts.

Alternatively just click Don't Show Again and it'll still work fine since the code already loads .env manually via load_dotenv() in both files.

