1:50 AM
The Kafka UI is showing status: initializing with brokerCount: 0 — it can't connect to the Kafka broker. This is a networking issue between the kafka-ui container and the kafka container.

Fix it by updating the kafka-ui service in your docker-compose.yml. Open it in VS Code and find the kafka-ui service, change this line:

yaml
KAFKA_CLUSTERS_0_BOOTSTRAPSERVERS: kafka:9092
Also add the networks section to make sure all containers can talk to each other. Here's the fixed docker-compose.yml:


Created a file, ran a command, read a file
Created a file, ran a command, read a file
Replace your existing docker-compose.yml with this one. The key fix is two listeners on Kafka:

PLAINTEXT://kafka:29092 — for internal container-to-container communication (Kafka UI uses this)
PLAINTEXT_HOST://localhost:9092 — for your Python scripts on the host machine
Then restart everything:

powershell
docker-compose down
docker-compose up -d
Wait 30 seconds, then open http://localhost:8080. The cluster should now show brokerCount: 1 and your raw-ticks topic. Let me know what you see!


Docker compose
YML 
I see them now. What was the fix?

1:53 AM
The fix was two separate Kafka listeners.

The Problem
Kafka was configured with only one listener:

yaml
KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS: PLAINTEXT://localhost:9092
localhost means different things depending on who is asking:

To your Python scripts on the host machine → localhost = your Windows PC ✅
To the Kafka UI container → localhost = itself (the Kafka UI container) ❌
So when Kafka UI tried to connect to localhost:9092, it was looking for Kafka inside its own container — which doesn't exist.

The Fix
We added two listeners on two different ports:

yaml
KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS: PLAINTEXT://kafka:29092,PLAINTEXT_HOST://localhost:9092
Listener	Address	Used by
PLAINTEXT	kafka:29092	Kafka UI and other containers — they use the container name kafka to find each other on the Docker network
PLAINTEXT_HOST	localhost:9092	Your Python scripts running on Windows host
And pointed Kafka UI to the internal listener:

yaml
KAFKA_CLUSTERS_0_BOOTSTRAPSERVERS: kafka:29092
In simple terms — containers talk to each other by container name, not localhost. Adding the shared marketpulse-network bridge ensured all containers could resolve each other's names.

